    <fr:title text="From Rust to Lean">From Rust to Lean</fr:title>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
    The step that connects a verified protocol to a prover people actually run. Extracting <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003I/" title="STARKs: Plonky3, Stwo, and Circle STARKs" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003I/" display-uri="dhsorens-003I" type="local">Plonky3</fr:link> into Lean and proving the extraction agrees with the specification is the current problem.
</html:p>
    <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>hax and its Lean backend: what Rust it accepts, and what it does with the parts it does not.</html:li>
        <html:li>The functional-equivalence obligation: extracted code against <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0041/" title="CompPoly" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0041/" display-uri="dhsorens-0041" type="local">CompPoly</fr:link>-level specifications.</html:li>
        <html:li>What is assumed rather than proved — unsafe blocks, arithmetic representations, parallelism.</html:li>
        <html:li>Keeping an extraction current against an upstream repository that moves weekly.</html:li>
        <html:li>Where proof automation helps, and what the AI-prover pipeline experience report actually reports.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
    <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.30106" type="external">A Rust-to-Lean Verification Pipeline with AI Provers</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/Overview" type="external">Verified-zkEVM overview</fr:link>.
</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
