                <fr:title text="Specifying the Machine: Sail, LLZK, zkLean">Specifying the Machine: Sail, LLZK, zkLean</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    The other half of a verified zkVM: a trustworthy specification of the machine being proved, and a way to reason about circuits written in the many DSLs that exist. <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0027/" title="LC3 in Lean" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0027/" display-uri="dhsorens-0027" type="local">LC3 in Lean</fr:link> is the same problem at a scale small enough to see all of it.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The Sail Lean backend: extracting the RISC-V specification into Lean, and what fidelity that gives.</html:li>
        <html:li>LLZK as an MLIR dialect unifying circuits from different DSLs — what it can and cannot express.</html:li>
        <html:li>zkLean for <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002S/" title="R1CS" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002S/" display-uri="dhsorens-002S" type="local">R1CS</fr:link>, alongside <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002Y/" title="Clean: Circuits and Their Specifications in Lean" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002Y/" display-uri="dhsorens-002Y" type="local">Clean</fr:link> for <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002U/" title="AIR" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002U/" display-uri="dhsorens-002U" type="local">AIR</fr:link>.</html:li>
        <html:li>The gap between an ISA specification and the constraint system claiming to implement it.</html:li>
        <html:li>Whether the EVM track's route (revm against KEVM, KEVM against a Lean model) is the right shape for the zkVM track too.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
                <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/Overview" type="external">Verified-zkEVM overview</fr:link>.
</html:p>
