          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    Computable polynomials and finite fields in Lean: an executable mirror of the Mathlib definitions, with transfer theorems so proofs can be done on the noncomputable side. The case for <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0025/" title="CompPoly and Computable Specifications" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0025/" display-uri="dhsorens-0025" type="local">computable specifications</fr:link> is made elsewhere; this page is about the artefact.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The representations chosen, and which operations each makes cheap. See <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002M/" title="Univariate and Multilinear Polynomials" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002M/" display-uri="dhsorens-002M" type="local">univariate and multilinear polynomials</fr:link>.</html:li>
        <html:li>The transfer pattern: computable definition, Mathlib definition, proven equivalence, and how proofs move across it.</html:li>
        <html:li>Field representations and the small fields that matter — see <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002K/" title="Finite Fields and the Small-Field Turn" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002K/" display-uri="dhsorens-002K" type="local">the small-field turn</fr:link>.</html:li>
        <html:li>What running the spec actually buys: testing a specification against a reference implementation.</html:li>
        <html:li>Performance in Lean, and whether it is adequate for testing at realistic trace sizes.</html:li>
        <html:li>Which <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0040/" title="ArkLib" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0040/" display-uri="dhsorens-0040" type="local">ArkLib</fr:link> developments depend on it.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
            <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/CompPoly" type="external">CompPoly</fr:link>.
</html:p>
